smithy scan: check a change's added lines and files for secrets

usage: smithy scan [--base <branch|ref>]
Checks the lines a change adds since the merge-base with the base (a branch name
means origin/<branch> when it exists; default origin's default branch),
uncommitted edits included, and the files it adds, for secrets and private keys,
git conflict markers (fenced code in docs exempt; a bare ======= only next to a
<<<<<<< or |||||||), and binary or archive files, printed as warnings for a
human look. Prints file:line, the rule and a masked match; exit 1 on any finding
(warnings don't count), 0 when clean. A line that is a known false positive can
carry "smithy:allow" to be skipped (only with the user's yes).

Examples:
  smithy scan
  smithy scan --base main

See also: smithy help receipt
Exit codes: 0 success, 1 failure, 2 usage error (3 where a command's help says).
